        P("The <b>minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC)</b> is the lowest drug concentration that stops growth. In disk diffusion, a drug disk sits on a lawn of bacteria and the clear <strong>zone</strong> around it is measured. A <b>breakpoint</b> is the MIC or zone value that sorts an isolate into a category. The <b>interpretive category</b> comes from the microbe, pharmacokinetic-pharmacodynamic data and clinical outcomes."),
        scale("Example breakpoints from the lecture", [("Susceptible", "MIC ≤ 4 · zone ≥ 20 mm", "ok"), ("Intermediate", "MIC 8 to 16 · zone 15 to 19 mm", "mid"), ("Resistant", "MIC ≥ 32 · zone ≤ 14 mm", "bad")]),
        callout("trap", P("MIC and zone run in opposite directions. A <strong>low</strong> MIC and a <strong>wide</strong> zone both mean susceptible.")),
